428015 1072 #uuid "39a1a8bd-f69f-5f36-99bc-5bd95f9b782d" 13194139534619 17592186045417 true] [17592186428015 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=9.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=81.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-81I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-81I=[\\begin{aligned}73 & 24 \\\\ 24 & 9\\end{aligned}]-81[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 24 \\\\ 24 & -72\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-81I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$" 13194139534619 17592186045417 true] [17592186428016 1058 #edb/ref 17592186428019 13194139534619 17592186045417 true] [17592186428016 1062 #uuid "0c463439-a38b-593e-b012-0bd4d757c8b7" 13194139534619 17592186045417 true] [17592186428016 1063 #edb/ref 17592186371360 13194139534619 17592186045417 true] [17592186428017 1058 #edb/ref 17592186428018 13194139534619 17592186045417 true] [17592186428017 1062 #uuid "42130988-8a9c-5c98-b57a-c5c217558433" 13194139534619 17592186045417 true] [17592186428017 1063 #edb/ref 17592186404836 13194139534619 17592186045417 true] [17592186428018 1058 #edb/ref 17592186428016 13194139534619 17592186045417 true] [17592186428018 1062 #uuid "b772e15d-f5b9-5724-9449-b771545c21bc" 13194139534619 17